  "policy": {
    "allowed_rights_bases": ["original", "us_federal_public_domain", "cc0"],
    "excluded": ["scraped", "cc_by", "cc_by_sa", "cc_nc", "cc_nd"],
    "cc_by_note": "excluded for MVP: attribution survival on the public surface is MVP-020's unbuilt work",
    "images": "none packaged; MVP-010 recipe photos was cut 2026-08-29",
    "authorship": "every entry below is written for this project. US law protects a recipe's literary expression, not its ingredient list or its functional steps; the instructions are written plainly and functionally on purpose, so no third-party text is reproduced and no attribution question arises.",
    "cook_review": "every entry is cook_review: null until a human cooks it. MVP-011 AC-3 says NOT VERIFIED cannot ship, and the installer only sees entries with a recorded review.",
    "expected_conflicts_note": "expected_conflicts records what food_domain::assess actually returns under rule version 1, not a nutritional claim. peanut-noodle-bowl therefore declares gluten: rule version 1 matches the term \"noodles\" in \"rice noodles\", which are gluten-free. The declaration is kept honest rather than dodged by renaming the ingredient — MVP-009 accepted explainable false positives, over-warning is the safe direction, and the warning copy never claims safety. The reverse direction is present too and is the one to watch: rule version 1 has no gluten term matching soy sauce, so vegetable-fried-rice declares soy and sesame but no gluten conflict, even though conventionally brewed soy sauce is made with wheat and is a well-known hidden gluten source. peanut-noodle-bowl carries the same soy sauce and is flagged gluten only because \"noodles\" matches inside \"rice noodles\", so the roster is inconsistent on this one ingredient, and inconsistent in the unsafe direction on one of the two. Adding soy sauce to the gluten terms — and deciding the same question for tamari and miso, which rule version 1 already matches as soy but not as gluten — is an MVP-009 rule change with a RULE_VERSION bump, not a content edit; renaming the ingredient to dodge the matcher is not an option here either.",
    "expected_conflicts_order": "order is not significant: every_recipe_matches_its_declared_conflicts sorts both sides into RestrictionKind::ALL order before comparing, so entries may list expected_conflicts in any order."
  },
